<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Fehrbellin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1675">1675</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scanian-war">Scanian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Fehrbellin was fought on 28 June 1675 near the town of Fehrbellin, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, between the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, the Great Elector</a>, and a Swedish army that had invaded the electorate. Brandenburg's cavalry, led in the attack by Frederick of Hesse-Homburg, defeated the Swedes. The victory ended the reputation of Swedish invincibility and raised the standing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fehrbellin.jpg" alt="Battle of Fehrbellin" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dismar Degen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_bei_Fehrbellin2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> had been allies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, but when Elector Frederick William joined Emperor Leopold I's expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> persuaded an isolated Sweden to attack Brandenburg while its army was away. The Swedes under Count Waldemar von Wrangel invaded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and occupied much of the electorate. Hearing of it in December 1674, Frederick William left the coalition, wintered at Marktbreit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, then, leaving his supply wagons and much of his infantry behind, marched 250 kilometres to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> in two weeks, a feat counted among the great marches in military history.</p><p>Finding the Swedes dispersed in the marshy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havelland">Havelland</a>, he sent Field Marshal Georg von Derfflinger to take Rathenow and split them. A banquet arranged by a bribed local official left the Swedish officers drunk, and Derfflinger, posing as a Swedish officer, had the gates opened. Wrangel fell back, his way blocked by marshes and by parties under Colonel Joachim Hennings, until he was cornered at Fehrbellin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>With the bridge over the Rhin destroyed and marshes on both flanks, Wrangel had to fight south of the village of Hakenberg while his engineers repaired it. About 6,000–7,000 Brandenburgers with 13 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> faced 7,000 Swedes with 28 guns, but Wrangel failed to secure the nearby heights, where the Brandenburgers placed their guns. Their fire caused heavy losses on the Swedish right, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> cavalry beat back every attempt to take the hills. The Swedish cavalry finally fled, and the infantry brigade of Generalmajor Henrik von Delwig was routed, but the bridge was repaired in time for Wrangel to bring much of his army across before dark.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Brandenburgers lost about 500 men and the Swedes some 500–600 killed, wounded and captured, but the retreating Swedes lost many more to pursuit and to local peasants; of Delwig's 1,200 men all but 20 were killed or captured. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> now declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and Frederick William invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a>, though the Peace of Saint-Germain-en-Laye of 1679 largely restored the status quo. In Sweden the defeat was laid against the Privy Council aristocrats at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> of 1680. Frederick William was known thereafter as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">the Great Elector</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-kleist">Heinrich von Kleist</a> made the battle the setting of his drama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince-of-homburg">The Prince of Homburg</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Fehrbellin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…standing army that grew to some 30,000 men, fought on both sides in the Second Northern War to win sovereignty over the Duchy of Prussia, and on 28 June 1675 defeated an invading Swedish army at the <b>Battle of Fehrbellin</b>, the first major victory of Brandenburg’s own forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick II, who lost a leg in Swedish service and commanded the Brandenburg cavalry at <b>Fehrbellin</b> in 1675, built the baroque palace and inspired Heinrich von Kleist's play The Prince of Homburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse-homburg">Landgraviate of Hesse-Homburg</a></li></ul></section>
